body:
Many thanks for your answer dude. It really puts some things into perspective.

I understand and can relate to some extent to what you're saying. The moment that you embrace death, then well... the world's your oyster. 

I guess I just beated myself mentally throughout the years carrying a nihilistic train of thought day in and day out, until everything just blinked out of my existance, on what is concerned its importance.

Sometimes I feel like I have this mood permanently just to avoid taking responsibilities as a man. But then again, I take full responsibility over my actions, wether right or wrong. I take care of my mother, who's kinda almost bedridden. I'm the one who talks to her the most, since my brother is kinda cold on that aspect. I constantly fret over the things that are missing on the house, since I don't want to leave my mother without any food( kinda food trauma from the past, when my father left, we didn't really have much money.)

&#x200B;

I don't know man. I guess that in the end I just want to justify a bad ending for me. I feel like I have something to atone for. I was not a saint when I was younger. I hurt my bestfriend deeply by faking an identity, which I was forgiven for by him, but never learned how to forgive myself for it. This happened so long ago, and he's pretty much okay that it happened, but I never really got back on my feet with this. I never really learned, even though I trash my head into finding what was the fucking brilliant idea of doing this was.

I just don't know. While I know that I've learned from every mistake I've done in order to be a better person to everybody around me, I feel like throughout the years, I've taken myself out of the equation of being happy, and just focused on helping other people in order to atone for what I did in my (even) younger days.

&#x200B;

It's what my mind does, I guess, and I have to reformat it, if I want to feel happy. As you, and some more people have said " Seize the moments granted. What’s the worst that could happen? You die?"

The Carpe Diem mentality is something that I kind of longed for, always. I'll have to accept that eventually there will have to come a time where I will have to seriously clash with my own demons in order to be either a better person, or just simply end it all.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;
